  "name": "Question 6",
  "problem": "Which of the following lines is perpendicular to $4x+2y=8?$",
  "worked_solution": "For the two lines to be perpendicular, their slopes must multiply to $-1.$\n\nTo find the slope of the given line $4x+2y=8,$ we convert to slope-intercept form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4x+2y & =8 \\\\ 2y & =-4x+8 \\\\ y & =-2x+4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn slope-intercept form, the slope is the $x$-coefficient. So, the slope of the given line is $-2.$\n\nLooking at the answer choices, the line $y=\\frac{1}{2}x+5$ has a slope of $\\frac{1}{2}.$\n\nMultiplying the slopes together, we have\n\n$$\n(-2)\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}=-1.\n$$\n\nSince the slopes multiply to $-1,$ we conclude that the line $y=\\frac{1}{2}x+5$ is perpendicular to the given line.",
